---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>You want to contribute to Pilot Protocol. You have cloned the repository, run <code>go build ./...</code>, and now you are staring at a tree of packages wondering where to start. This article is the map. It covers the main repositories and packages, explains how the test environment works, walks through adding a new service from scratch, explains the layering rule CI enforces, and suggests where to start.</p>

  <p>Pilot Protocol is a pure-Go project: no cgo, no code generation, and <code>go test</code> just works. The core module's direct third-party dependencies are few (<code>coder/websocket</code> and <code>golang.org/x/sys</code>, plus the project's own modules); <code>go.mod</code> lists what they pull in indirectly. The code is split across several repositories under <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/pilot-protocol</a>, each its own Go module, so you can read one part without holding the whole thing in your head.</p>

  <section>
  <h2>The Repository Map</h2>

  <p>The daemon and CLI live in <code>pilotprotocol</code>. Shared types live in <code>common</code>. The registry, the beacon and each daemon plugin are separate repositories that <code>pilotprotocol</code> imports as Go modules. Here is what each piece does and where the important files live.</p>

  <h3>common/protocol - Addresses, Headers, CRC</h3>

  <p>This is the foundation layer. It defines the 48-bit virtual address format (<code>[16-bit network][32-bit node]</code>, text format <code>N:NNNN.HHHH.LLLL</code>), the 34-byte packet header structure, the well-known ports, and CRC32 checksum computation. Nearly every other package imports <code>protocol</code>. If you are fixing a serialization bug or adding a new message type, this is where you start. The address parsing is strict, and the text format must match exactly. The header includes fields for source/destination addresses, port numbers, sequence and acknowledgment numbers, flags, window size, and checksum. All multi-byte fields are big-endian.</p>

  <h3>pilotprotocol/pkg/daemon - The Core Engine</h3>

  <p>The daemon is the heart of Pilot Protocol. It manages the UDP tunnel, handles NAT traversal on the client side, runs the port manager, processes IPC commands from the CLI and SDKs, and coordinates encryption. This is the largest package and the one you will spend the most time reading. Key files handle tunnel read/write loops (<code>tunnel.go</code>), connection state machines (SYN/ACK/FIN lifecycle) and sliding window transport with congestion control, flow control with advertised receive windows, Nagle-style coalescing of small writes, segmentation (<code>ports.go</code>), keepalive probes, idle timeouts, and graceful shutdown. If you are working on transport reliability, congestion behavior, or connection lifecycle, this is the package.</p>

  <h3>rendezvous - The Registry</h3>

  <p>The registry is the rendezvous server's brain, in its own repository. It handles node registration, address assignment, hostname resolution, networks and their roles and policies, persistence (snapshots plus a write-ahead log), and hot-standby replication. Its <code>cmd/rendezvous</code> binary runs the registry and a beacon together; use <code>-registry-addr</code> and <code>-beacon-addr</code> to set their listen addresses. This binary is what you deploy if you run your own rendezvous. If you are working on discovery, node management, or multi-server coordination, start here.</p>

  <h3>beacon - STUN, Hole-Punch, Relay</h3>

  <p>The beacon handles the server side of NAT traversal. It answers STUN-style discovery requests so daemons learn their external address, coordinates UDP hole-punching (sending <code>BeaconMsgPunchCommand</code> to both peers), and relays traffic when no direct path works. Relayed packets travel in <code>BeaconMsgRelay</code> frames with the format <code>[0x05][senderNodeID(4)][destNodeID(4)][payload...]</code>; the payload stays encrypted end to end. The beacon can listen dual-stack (IPv4 and IPv6). The daemon's fallback chain (direct, hole-punch, relay) spans the daemon and the beacon, so changes here require careful testing across NAT setups.</p>

  <h3>common/driver - IPC Client and Go SDK</h3>

  <p>The driver is the Go SDK that applications use to talk to the daemon. It connects over a Unix domain socket (<code>/tmp/pilot.sock</code> by default) and provides an API for dialing and listening on ports, sending datagrams, resolving hostnames, and managing trust. It serializes IPC frame writes with a mutex so concurrent goroutines don't interleave frames. The <code>Conn</code> type implements <code>net.Conn</code>, with working read deadlines, and <code>Listen</code> returns a <code>net.Listener</code>, which is what lets <code>http.Serve</code> run over Pilot. If you are building an application on Pilot, the driver is your entry point.</p>

  <h3>pilotprotocol/cmd/pilotctl - The CLI</h3>

  <p>The command-line interface. It wraps the driver with commands like <code>info</code>, <code>ping</code>, <code>connect</code>, <code>send-message</code>, <code>inbox</code>, <code>send-file</code>, <code>publish</code>/<code>subscribe</code>, <code>handshake</code>/<code>approve</code>/<code>trust</code>, <code>bench</code>, <code>lookup</code>, and the <code>appstore</code> commands. Most commands are thin wrappers that parse flags, call the driver, and format output (text, or JSON with <code>--json</code>). Adding a new CLI command is one of the easiest contributions. Commands like <code>set-hostname</code>, <code>set-public</code>/<code>set-private</code>, and <code>deregister</code> go through the daemon IPC (not directly to the registry) because the daemon signs them with the node's key.</p>

  <h3>Plugins: dataexchange, eventstream, handshake, policy, webhook</h3>

  <p>The built-in services are daemon plugins, each in its own repository. A plugin implements <code>coreapi.Service</code> from <code>common/coreapi</code> (<code>Name</code>, <code>Order</code>, <code>Start</code>, <code>Stop</code>); <code>Start</code> receives the daemon's capabilities, including <code>Streams</code> for listening on and dialing ports. <code>dataexchange</code> (port 1001) carries messages and files and writes them to the inbox and <code>~/.pilot/received/</code>. <code>eventstream</code> (port 1002) is the pub/sub broker. <code>handshake</code> runs trust handshakes, <code>policy</code> evaluates network policies, and <code>webhook</code> delivers event notifications. <code>pilotprotocol/cmd/daemon/main.go</code> registers them with the plugin <code>runtime</code>. The echo service on port 7 is built into the daemon itself.</p>

  <h3>tests/ - Integration Tests</h3>

  <p>The integration test suite in <code>pilotprotocol/tests</code> uses <code>testenv.go</code> to spin up a full in-process environment: beacon, registry, and multiple daemons with the standard plugins, all running in the same process with real UDP sockets. This gives you end-to-end testing without deploying anything. Run <code>go test -parallel 4 -count=1 ./tests/</code> for the current tally. The test files are organized by feature with a <code>zz_</code> prefix: <code>zz_handshake_test.go</code>, <code>zz_privacy_test.go</code>, <code>zz_nat_traversal_test.go</code>, <code>zz_webhook_test.go</code>, and so on. Every PR should include tests, and most features can be tested entirely through testenv without any external infrastructure.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>The Test Environment</h2>

  <p>The test environment is the single most important file for contributors to understand. <code>tests/testenv.go</code> creates a miniature Pilot network inside a single Go test process. Here is how to use it:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Run all integration tests (always use -parallel 4)</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 ./tests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Run a specific test</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 -run TestHandshake ./tests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Run with verbose output</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 -v -run TestHandshake ./tests/</span></code></pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Critical:</strong> Always use <code>-parallel 4</code>. Unlimited parallelism exhausts ports and sockets, causing dial timeouts and flaky test failures. This is not a suggestion; CI runs with it too.</p>
  </div>

  <p><code>NewTestEnv</code> starts a beacon and a registry on random available ports, and <code>AddDaemon</code> starts as many daemons as the test needs. Each daemon gets its own virtual address, its own IPC socket, and its own tunnel. The daemons communicate over real UDP on localhost, so you are testing the actual network path, not mocks. Test daemons are public, so they can talk without a trust handshake unless the test is about trust.</p>

  <p>A typical test follows this pattern:</p>

  <pre><code>func TestEchoRoundTrip(t *testing.T) {
    t.Parallel()

    <span class="comment">// Beacon + registry; cleaned up automatically when the test ends</span>
    env := NewTestEnv(t)
    a := env.AddDaemon()
    b := env.AddDaemon()

    <span class="comment">// Open a connection from A to B's built-in echo service (port 7)</span>
    conn, err := a.Driver.DialAddr(b.Daemon.Addr(), protocol.PortEcho)
    if err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("dial echo: %v", err)
    }
    defer conn.Close()

    <span class="comment">// Send data and verify the echo</span>
    conn.Write([]byte("hello"))
    buf := make([]byte, 5)
    if _, err := io.ReadFull(conn, buf); err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("read: %v", err)
    }
    if string(buf) != "hello" {
        t.Fatalf("echo = %q, want hello", buf)
    }
}</code></pre>

  <p>The environment handles all the setup: STUN discovery, registry registration, tunnel establishment, and cleanup. Your test just describes the scenario and checks the result.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Adding a New Service: A Walkthrough</h2>

  <p>Let us walk through adding a hypothetical new service on port 1004. Say you want a "latency" service that responds with one-way latency measurements. Here is the complete process.</p>

  <h3>Step 1: Write the Plugin</h3>

  <p>Every service is a plugin that implements <code>coreapi.Service</code>. The built-in plugins each live in their own Go module (look at <code>eventstream/service.go</code> for a complete one); a new plugin can start the same way. <code>Start</code> listens on the service's port and handles each incoming stream:</p>

  <pre><code>package latency

import (
    "context"
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
    "io"
    "time"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/coreapi"
)

<span class="comment">// PortLatency is the port the latency service listens on.</span>
const PortLatency uint16 = 1004

<span class="comment">// Response is the JSON payload returned to the caller.</span>
type Response struct {
    ReceivedAt time.Time     \`json:"received_at"\`
    Latency    time.Duration \`json:"latency_ns"\`
}

<span class="comment">// Service is the daemon plugin.</span>
type Service struct{ ln coreapi.Listener }

func (s *Service) Name() string { return "latency" }
func (s *Service) Order() int   { return 200 }

<span class="comment">// Start is called by the plugin runtime with the daemon's capabilities.</span>
func (s *Service) Start(ctx context.Context, deps coreapi.Deps) error {
    ln, err := deps.Streams.Listen(PortLatency)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("latency: listen on port %d: %w", PortLatency, err)
    }
    s.ln = ln
    go func() {
        for {
            st, err := ln.Accept()
            if err != nil {
                return
            }
            go handle(st)
        }
    }()
    return nil
}

func (s *Service) Stop(ctx context.Context) error {
    if s.ln == nil {
        return nil
    }
    return s.ln.Close()
}

<span class="comment">// handle answers each timestamp the peer sends with the measured latency.</span>
func handle(conn io.ReadWriteCloser) {
    defer conn.Close()
    buf := make([]byte, 1024)
    for {
        n, err := conn.Read(buf)
        if err != nil {
            return
        }
        received := time.Now()

        <span class="comment">// Parse the timestamp sent by the caller</span>
        var sent time.Time
        if err := json.Unmarshal(buf[:n], &sent); err != nil {
            return
        }

        <span class="comment">// Respond with latency measurement</span>
        resp := Response{
            ReceivedAt: received,
            Latency:    received.Sub(sent),
        }
        data, _ := json.Marshal(resp)
        if _, err := conn.Write(data); err != nil {
            return
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

  <h3>Step 2: Register with the Daemon</h3>

  <p>In <code>cmd/daemon/main.go</code>, register the plugin with the runtime alongside the others. The runtime starts plugins by <code>Order</code> and stops them in reverse registration order on shutdown.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment">// In cmd/daemon/main.go, next to the dataexchange and eventstream registrations:</span>
if err := rt.Register(&latency.Service{}); err != nil {
    log.Fatalf("register latency: %v", err)
}</code></pre>

  <h3>Step 3: Add the CLI Command</h3>

  <p>In <code>cmd/pilotctl</code>, add a <code>latency</code> subcommand that connects to port 1004 on a target, sends a timestamp, and reads back the response (<code>connectDriver</code> and <code>parseAddrOrHostname</code> are existing helpers there):</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment">// pilotctl latency &lt;address|hostname&gt;</span>
func cmdLatency(args []string) {
    d := connectDriver()
    defer d.Close()
    target, err := parseAddrOrHostname(d, args[0])
    if err != nil {
        fatalCode("not_found", "%v", err)
    }
    conn, err := d.DialAddr(target, latency.PortLatency)
    if err != nil {
        fatalCode("connection_failed", "dial: %v", err)
    }
    defer conn.Close()

    data, _ := json.Marshal(time.Now())
    conn.Write(data)

    buf := make([]byte, 1024)
    n, _ := conn.Read(buf)

    var resp latency.Response
    json.Unmarshal(buf[:n], &resp)
    fmt.Printf("Latency: %v\\n", resp.Latency)
}</code></pre>

  <h3>Step 4: Write the Test</h3>

  <p>The handler only needs a stream, so a unit test can drive it over an in-memory pipe:</p>

  <pre><code>func TestHandleReportsLatency(t *testing.T) {
    client, server := net.Pipe()
    defer client.Close()
    go handle(server)

    <span class="comment">// Send timestamp</span>
    data, _ := json.Marshal(time.Now())
    if _, err := client.Write(data); err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("write: %v", err)
    }

    <span class="comment">// Read response</span>
    buf := make([]byte, 1024)
    n, err := client.Read(buf)
    if err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("read: %v", err)
    }

    var resp Response
    if err := json.Unmarshal(buf[:n], &resp); err != nil {
        t.Fatalf("decode: %v", err)
    }
    if resp.ReceivedAt.IsZero() || resp.Latency < 0 {
        t.Fatalf("bad response: %+v", resp)
    }
}</code></pre>

  <p>For an end-to-end test, register the plugin in <code>registerStandardPlugins</code> in <code>tests/testenv.go</code> and dial port 1004 from a second daemon, the way the existing tests exercise the built-in services. That is it: plugin, registration, CLI, test. Look at <code>dataexchange</code> or <code>eventstream</code> for real-world examples of more complex service protocols.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Layering and Linting</h2>

  <p>The project enforces a layered architecture. <code>layers.yaml</code> in <code>pilotprotocol</code> assigns every package to a layer (datagram I/O, routing, transport, services, and so on), and imports must always point downward. The <code>check-layers</code> tool reads that file and fails CI on any violation, so if your change adds an import across layers, expect to restructure it rather than suppress the check.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> Run <code>go vet ./...</code> and <code>go test -parallel 4 -count=1 ./tests/</code> before every commit. <code>make lint</code> runs <code>golangci-lint</code> if you have it installed.</p>
  </div>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Where to Start</h2>

  <p>Check the open issues in the GitHub repositories first. If nothing there fits, these kinds of change are self-contained and touch one area each:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>A new CLI command or flag.</strong> Commands in <code>cmd/pilotctl</code> are thin wrappers over the driver; add the usage text to the <code>commandHelp</code> map alongside the code.</li>
    <li><strong>More test coverage.</strong> Pick a feature with thin tests in <code>tests/</code> (broadcast in <code>zz_broadcast_test.go</code>, for example) and add cases through testenv.</li>
    <li><strong>Better error messages.</strong> Walk through the daemon's connection establishment code and add context to errors: which address was being dialed, which port, whether the peer was resolved. Wrapping errors with <code>fmt.Errorf("dial %s port %d: %w", addr, port, err)</code> makes debugging much easier, and it is a good way to learn the codebase.</li>
  </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Development Workflow</h2>

  <p>Here is the workflow that regular contributors use:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Clone and verify the build</span>
<span class="cmd">git clone https://github.com/pilot-protocol/pilotprotocol.git</span>
<span class="cmd">cd pilotprotocol</span>
<span class="cmd">go build ./...</span>

<span class="comment"># Run the full test suite</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 ./tests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Make your changes, then verify</span>
<span class="cmd">go vet ./...</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 ./tests/</span>

<span class="comment"># Run just the test for your feature</span>
<span class="cmd">go test -parallel 4 -count=1 -v -run TestYourFeature ./tests/</span></code></pre>

  <p>The project has few direct dependencies and no code generation steps. The <code>Makefile</code> provides convenience targets (<code>make build</code> puts the daemon and <code>pilotctl</code> in <code>bin/</code>), but <code>go build</code> and <code>go test</code> are all you need.</p>

  <p>For larger changes, read the <a href="/docs/">documentation</a> and technical specification linked from the <a href="/docs/research">research page</a>, which describes the wire protocol and message types. If your change modifies protocol behavior, note it in your PR.</p>

  <p>Every contribution goes through standard GitHub PR review. Keep PRs focused: one feature or fix per PR. Include tests. Update the spec if you change protocol behavior. And always run the full test suite with <code>-parallel 4</code> before pushing.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Where to Go Next</h2>

  <p>If you want to understand the protocol itself before diving into code, read <a href="how-pilot-protocol-works">How Pilot Protocol Works</a> for the architecture overview. For NAT traversal specifically, the <a href="nat-traversal-ai-agents-deep-dive">NAT traversal deep dive</a> explains how the beacon coordinates hole punching and relay. For the trust and encryption model, see <a href="zero-dependency-encryption-x25519-aes-gcm">Zero-Dependency Agent Encryption</a> and <a href="trust-model-agents-invisible-by-default">The Trust Model</a>.</p>

  <p>The codebase is deliberately plain. No frameworks, no generated code, few direct dependencies. Just Go, the standard library, and the problem. Because each piece is its own module with strict layering, you can understand the part you are changing without reading everything else.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="cta">
    <h3>Start Contributing</h3>
    <p>Clone the repo, run the tests, pick an issue.</p>
    <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/pilotprotocol">View on GitHub</a>
  </div>`;
